1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,130
Lesson 10.9.

2
00:00:02,510 --> 00:00:06,886
Case study: an AFT
language model end to end.

3
00:00:08,017 --> 00:00:12,110
We close the unit with a
real case study, end to end.

4
00:00:12,490 --> 00:00:16,108
We train a small language model,
of about one hundred thirteen

5
00:00:16,108 --> 00:00:19,260
thousand parameters, on the
very text of this course's

6
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:22,937
narrations: three hundred forty
thousand characters in Spanish.

7
00:00:23,317 --> 00:00:27,337
We use all the pieces of the unit
and measure four variants with

8
00:00:27,337 --> 00:00:30,980
the same data, the same seed
and the same number of steps.

9
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:34,488
The model has two blocks of dimension

10
00:00:34,488 --> 00:00:37,867
sixty-four, with a context
of sixty-four characters.

11
00:00:38,247 --> 00:00:42,372
Mixing is causal AFT with
learned local biases in

12
00:00:42,372 --> 00:00:45,907
a window of four positions, like AFT-conv.

13
00:00:46,287 --> 00:00:50,550
Normalization is DyT and
the MLP uses tangent.

14
00:00:50,930 --> 00:00:54,407
For masked diffusion,
mixing is bidirectional:

15
00:00:54,407 --> 00:00:57,808
the average of a forward
and a backward pass.

16
00:00:58,917 --> 00:01:00,320
A lesson from the process.

17
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:03,633
In the first version, the
diffusion model learned

18
00:01:03,633 --> 00:01:06,268
nothing beyond the
frequency of each letter.

19
00:01:06,648 --> 00:01:11,414
The cause: a masked position only receives
information from its neighbors, and

20
00:01:11,414 --> 00:01:15,937
with global mixing that information
is diluted among sixty-four positions.

21
00:01:16,317 --> 00:01:20,290
The gradient of the local
biases was too noisy to grow.

22
00:01:20,670 --> 00:01:25,028
Initializing those biases with a
preference for nearby neighbors solved it.

23
00:01:25,408 --> 00:01:27,050
Initialization matters.

24
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:30,518
The results, in bits per character on

25
00:01:30,518 --> 00:01:33,333
validation text, after
three thousand steps.

26
00:01:33,713 --> 00:01:36,799
Autoregressive with Adam: 1.98.

27
00:01:37,179 --> 00:01:42,598
With natural gradient, solved with
conjugate gradient: 1.94, and at

28
00:01:42,598 --> 00:01:47,532
five hundred steps the advantage
was larger, 2.2 versus 2.32.

29
00:01:47,912 --> 00:01:53,373
With sparse masks of degree
four: 2.32, the sparsity tax.

30
00:01:53,753 --> 00:01:59,682
And masked diffusion gives a bound of
3.18: at this scale, clearly worse.

31
00:02:00,783 --> 00:02:04,805
Natural gradient wins per step,
but on the GPU it took fifty-two

32
00:02:04,805 --> 00:02:09,078
seconds against nine and a half for
Adam, because each step solves a

33
00:02:09,078 --> 00:02:12,597
linear system with ten
iterations of conjugate gradient.

34
00:02:12,977 --> 00:02:16,521
That solve is exactly
what TNGD delegates to a

35
00:02:16,521 --> 00:02:20,141
thermodynamic circuit,
as we saw in lesson 8.3.

36
00:02:20,521 --> 00:02:23,282
If the analog solve were fast and cheap,

37
00:02:23,282 --> 00:02:26,250
natural gradient would
also win per second.

38
00:02:27,383 --> 00:02:28,769
We log the energy.

39
00:02:29,149 --> 00:02:34,003
The dense model costs about two
hundred seven thousand operations per

40
00:02:34,003 --> 00:02:38,857
token; at ten percent utilization
and 0.177 picojoules per operation,

41
00:02:39,237 --> 00:02:43,669
about three hundred sixty-six
nanojoules on the GPU, an estimate.

42
00:02:44,049 --> 00:02:48,446
The degree-four model uses twenty-one
times fewer weights in the projections.

43
00:02:48,826 --> 00:02:52,738
Its mapping record: four
thousand six hundred eight

44
00:02:52,738 --> 00:02:57,034
pbits, degree sixteen, eight
sequential sampling layers;

45
00:02:57,034 --> 00:03:01,022
AFT mixing, the embedding
and the head stay digital.

46
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:04,902
The code is in the lesson's data folder.

47
00:03:05,282 --> 00:03:10,471
You can swap the optimizer, including
a TNGD simulator, and log in each

48
00:03:10,471 --> 00:03:15,148
run bits per character, time,
operations and the mapping record.

49
00:03:15,528 --> 00:03:18,469
In labs twenty-eight
and twenty-nine you will

50
00:03:18,469 --> 00:03:21,476
extend it with sampled
layers and dy4p inputs.

51
00:03:22,583 --> 00:03:25,667
Common mistakes: comparing
the autoregressive

52
00:03:25,667 --> 00:03:29,503
likelihood with a diffusion
bound as if both were exact.

53
00:03:29,883 --> 00:03:33,534
Judging natural gradient only
by its time on digital hardware.

54
00:03:33,914 --> 00:03:36,906
Changing seeds, data or
steps between variants.

55
00:03:37,286 --> 00:03:39,924
And reporting energy without
utilization or scope.

56
00:03:41,050 --> 00:03:45,584
In summary, a small AFT model
with DyT and tangent learns

57
00:03:45,584 --> 00:03:49,403
the course text down to
1.98 bits per character.

58
00:03:49,783 --> 00:03:55,153
Natural gradient wins per step, and
TNGD could make it win per second.

59
00:03:55,533 --> 00:03:58,688
Degree-four sparsity costs
a third of a bit, and

60
00:03:58,688 --> 00:04:01,580
masked diffusion falls
behind at this scale.

61
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:05,977
Everything is logged: quality,
time, operations and mapping.

62
00:04:07,083 --> 00:04:11,522
To practice: explain why the first
diffusion only learned frequencies.

63
00:04:11,902 --> 00:04:15,427
Compute the weight ratio between
the dense and the sparse model.

64
00:04:15,807 --> 00:04:18,501
Say what would make natural
gradient win in time.

65
00:04:18,881 --> 00:04:21,874
And explain why the
diffusion figure is a bound.
